course: world
lesson: 128
title: Growing up: ages of people and pets, and the "dog years" myth
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Living things age at very different speeds. A hamster is old at 2; a mayfly lives only about a day as a flying adult, after a year or more as a nymph underwater; a cat often lives 12 to 18 years; and a tortoise can outlive its owner's whole family: Jonathan, on the island of St Helena, is thought to have hatched around 1832.
explain: "Times 7" for dog years is a myth. Dogs grow up very fast at first, so a 1-year-old dog is already like a teenager, and then they age more slowly; big dogs age faster than small ones. A vets' guide for a middle-sized dog says year 1 is like about 15 human years, year 2 brings it to about 24, then add about 5 for each year after: a 3-year-old dog is like about 28 or 29, not 21.
explain: Cats work the same way with 4 a year after the second: a 10-year-old cat is like a person of about 56, middle-aged.
explain: Children grow at their own speeds, often about 5 to 6 cm a year before puberty (when a child's body starts changing into a grown-up's), with growth spurts that come especially in the teens; sleep, food and play all help. You can guess with a simple sum: 20 cm to go at 5 cm a year takes 4 years.
explain: Bodies keep time too: a child's heart beats about 80 times a minute, a mouse's about 500 and an elephant's about 30. Growing up brings new things you're allowed to do, like learning to drive at 17 in Britain, but being on time stays your job all your life.
fact: The old "times 7" rule for dog years is a myth.
fact: A vets' guide for a middle-sized dog: the first year is like about 15 human years, by the second year about 24, then about 5 more for each year after.
fact: By that guide a 3-year-old dog is like a person of about 28 or 29, and big dogs age faster than small ones.
fact: For cats the first year is like about 15, the second brings it to about 24, then about 4 a year, so a 10-year-old cat is like about 24 + 8 × 4 = 56.
fact: Cats often live 12 to 18 years, and hamsters about 2 to 3 years.
fact: Some tortoises live over 100 years; Jonathan, on St Helena, is thought to have hatched around 1832, about 194 years before 2026.
fact: An adult mayfly lives about a day, after a year or more as a nymph underwater.
fact: Children often grow about 5 to 6 cm a year before puberty, and growth spurts happen, especially in the teen years.
fact: Holly, 140 cm, growing about 5 cm a year, needs 20 ÷ 5 = 4 years to reach 160 cm.
fact: A child's heart beats about 80 times a minute, which is 80 × 60 = 4,800 times an hour.
fact: Small animals' hearts usually beat faster: a mouse's can beat about 500 times a minute and an elephant's about 30.
fact: 10 is the first age with two digits.
fact: In Britain you can start learning to drive a car at 17.
exercise: What do vets say about the "times 7" rule for dog years? | It's a myth: dogs age fast at first, then slower | It's exactly right for every dog | It should be times 10, not times 7 | It's right for big dogs only
exercise: By the vets' guide, a 3-year-old middle-sized dog is like a person of about... | 28 or 29 | 21 | 3 | 70
exercise: Which dogs age faster? | Big dogs | Small dogs | Short-haired dogs | All dogs age the same
exercise: How long do hamsters usually live? | About 2 to 3 years | About 10 to 15 years | About 2 to 3 months | About 50 years, like a person
exercise: A 10-year-old cat is like a person of about... | 56 | 70 | 10 | 30
exercise: How long do cats often live? | 12 to 18 years | 2 to 3 years | 50 to 60 years | 4 to 5 years
exercise: About how old is Jonathan the tortoise thought to be in 2026? | About 194 years | About 94 years | About 1,832 years | About 50 years
exercise: How long does an adult mayfly live? | About a day | About a year | About a minute | About ten years
exercise: Where does a mayfly spend most of its life? | Underwater as a nymph | Flying in the air | Inside a flower | Underground as a worm
exercise: Mia grew 4 cm and her brother grew 6 cm. How much more did he grow? | 2 cm | 10 cm | 4 cm | 24 cm
exercise: About how much do children often grow each year before puberty? | About 5 to 6 cm | About 20 cm | About 1 mm | About 1 metre
exercise: At what age do you first have "double digits"? | 10 | 2 | 11 | 100
exercise: About how many times does a child's heart beat in an hour? | 4,800 | 80 | 480 | 48,000
exercise: Whose heart beats fastest? | A mouse's | An elephant's | A young child's | A big horse's
exercise: Holly is 140 cm and Gran is 160 cm. Holly grows about 5 cm a year. How many years until she is as tall as Gran? | 4 | 20 | 5 | 13
exercise: At what age can you start learning to drive in Britain? | 17 | 10 | 21 | 13
test: Jack: "My dog's 3, so he's 21 in dog years, simple maths." Vet: "That's a myth, actually." What's closer? | About 28 or 29, because dogs grow up fast at first | Exactly 21, so the vet has got it completely wrong | About 3, because dogs age just the same as people do | About 70, because dogs age very fast from the start
test: Ruby: "My hamster's 2. Two isn't old!" What's true? | For a hamster, 2 is old; they live about 2 to 3 years | Hamsters live about 20 years, so it's a baby | Hamsters never get old | A hamster is only old after 10
test: Ben's grandad: "This tortoise was my dad's, and it'll see me out, I reckon." ("See me out" means outlive me.) Is that possible? | Yes, some tortoises live over 100 years | No, tortoises live about 10 years | No, pets always die before their owners | Only if the tortoise never sleeps in winter
test: Lily: "Cat's 10. Is that, like, old?" Mum: "Kind of middle-aged." Why? | By the cat guide, 10 is like about 56 for a person | 10 is like 70, so very old | 10 is like 10 for a person | Cats are already old by the time they reach about 3 years
test: Kai: "Mayfly lives one day? That's grim, bro." ("Grim" means sad.) What's he missing? | It lives a year or more as a nymph first | It lives one day and is never young at all | It lives a thousand years deep underground | It comes back to life again the very next day
test: Mia: "He grew 6 cm and I only grew 4, it's well unfair!" What's the best answer? | Children grow at different speeds, and spurts come at different times | She must be ill, since everyone grows exactly the same amount every year | Boys always grow exactly 2 cm more a year | She will never grow again after 9
test: Gran, from Newcastle: "You'll be taller than me by 13, pet." ("Pet" means love.) Holly is 9 and 140 cm; Gran is 160 cm. At about 5 cm a year, is Gran right? | Yes: 20 cm at 5 cm a year takes 4 years, to age 13 | No: it would take 20 years | No: Holly will be 13 cm taller | Yes, but only if Gran shrinks 20 cm
test: Ethan's mum: "Double digits now, our kid!" ("Our kid" means my lad, in the North.) How old is Ethan? | 10 | 2 | 11 | 12
test: Which would you expect: a tiny shrew's heart beating faster or slower than a horse's? | Faster: small animals' hearts usually beat faster | Slower: small animals' hearts beat slower | The same: all hearts beat at one speed | Slower: small hearts need less blood
test: Max: "When I'm 18, I can do whatever I want, fr fr." ("Fr fr" means for real.) Dad: "You'll still have to be on time for work." Who's right? | Dad: some rules change with age, but being on time stays | Max: at 18 there are no rules at all | Max: grown-ups never need to be on time | Dad: at 18 you can't do anything new
test: A cartoon dog says, "I'm 7 in dog years because I'm 1!" Fix the joke. | A 1-year-old dog is more like a 15-year-old person | A 1-year-old dog is more like a 1-year-old person, a toddler | A 1-year-old dog is more like a 70-year-old grandparent | A 1-year-old dog is still a tiny newborn puppy, eyes shut
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the rule whereby the age of a dog is multiplied by seven? | It is an oversimplification, dogs maturing rapidly at first and more slowly thereafter | It is exact for dogs of every size and breed | It applies to cats but not to dogs | It was established by law in the year 1907
exam: Given that a cat's first year corresponds to fifteen human years, its second to a total of twenty-four, and each subsequent year to four, ascertain the human equivalent of a cat aged ten years. | Fifty-six | Seventy | Forty | Sixty-four
exam: A child of 140 centimetres grows at a rate of five centimetres per annum. Determine the number of years required to attain 160 centimetres. | Four | Twenty | Five | Three
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the heart rates of animals. | Smaller animals generally possess faster heart rates than larger animals | Larger animals generally possess faster heart rates than smaller animals | All mammals possess identical heart rates | Heart rate bears no relation to the size of the animal
